<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodor Mommsen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1817">1817</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Christian Matthias Theodor Mommsen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodor Mommsen was a German classical scholar, historian, jurist and politician, born in Garding in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. After studying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>, he taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, Zurich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a> and from 1861 was professor of Roman History at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a>. He planned and led the Corpus Inscriptionum Latinarum, a collection of all known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscriptions checked against the originals, and pioneered the study of epigraphy. His Römische Geschichte (History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>) won him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1902, and he sat in the Prussian and German parliaments. He published over 1,500 works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodor-mommsen.jpg" alt="Theodor Mommsen" width="330" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodor_Mommsen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
